WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our next guest is
a talented actress

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.934
who wrote, produced and, stars
in "Shelter in Solitude",

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:06.801
which will be in theaters
October 6th.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:09.868
Let's take a look.
-Normally,

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:14.567
the way a conversation goes
is, "Do you have children?

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:18.400
You have kids?"
"No. Do you, Valerie?"

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.801
"No, I don't, Jackson."
"Why don't you, Valerie?"

00:00:21.801 --> 00:00:23.968
"Oh, because...

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:29.667
I never married."

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:34.334
-Why not?

00:00:40.501 --> 00:00:42.300
-I don't know.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.100
My daddy always said it was
'cause I intimidated the boys

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.734
'cause I had
unearned effervescence.

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:51.968
-Please welcome to the show,
Siobhan Fallon Hogan.

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:54.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.968 --> 00:01:00.100
♪♪

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.167
♪♪

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.834
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:09.200
-I am thrilled to be here, Seth.
I'm thrilled.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.868
-So you used to work
in this building

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:12.167
at a "Saturday Night Live".

00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:14.067
Have you been back since then?
-I just went over there,

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.434
and I said,
"Does anybody remember me?"

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:16.634
And they said,
"What year are you here?"

00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:22.100
I said, "20- and, uh,
20- and -2000 years ago."

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.868
-[ Laughs ]
And they hate doing the math.

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:25.868
-They're like, "Who are ya?"
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:28.167
-I actually said, "1902",
but I forgot my joke.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:32.133
-So now you --
you wrote, you produced,

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:33.501
and you starred in this movie.
-Yes.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:34.968
-I know how hard it can be

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:36.701
to make an independent film
like this.

00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:39.167
How long did it take the process
from start to finish?

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.601
-Start to finish? Okay.

00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:42.133
I was laying in bed one night
in the middle of the night.

00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:43.300
I had written
a movie called "Rushed",

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.200
which came out two years ago,

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.334
and I said I'm taking a break
because I'm elderly.

00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:49.934
And so, I -- then this idea
came to me

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:50.934
in the middle of the night.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:52.868
My dad had been an attorney --

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:55.567
I'm from five kids,
I'm the second of five --

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:57.334
and he used to tell us
about these prisoners,

00:01:57.334 --> 00:01:59.567
that he represented,
at our -- at our kitchen table.

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.968
And I'd be kind of, like,
terrified but really fascinated.

00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:04.067
And so, he also wanted me
to be a country

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.467
[Voice raises] singer,

00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:07.100
[Normal voice]
but I didn't have the voice.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.167
-Oh, I thought the way
you said "singer" was beautiful.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.167
-You like that?
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:12.133
-I'm affected. So... Okay.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.601
So, I have red nails.
I got it going on for the show.

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:15.634
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-So, okay...

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:17.200
So anyway, so this idea
came to me

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.334
in the middle of the night,
and I said,

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:22.634
"What if a prisoner
has 10 days left to live

00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:26.534
and a washed up country singer
becomes his guard?"

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:28.267
And so, that's where
the idea came from.

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:29.934
And I said, "Wait a minute,
I got to have Robert Patrick

00:02:29.934 --> 00:02:31.601
in there", you know,
from "The Terminator".

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:34.167
He's fabulous, and he played
my husband in "Rushed".

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:36.400
I was like,
"We need a hard ass warden."

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.133
And so --
I wasn't supposed to swear.

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:39.234
Sorry.
-That's okay.

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:42.000
-And so, anyway,
that's where it came from.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.634
And then I wrote it
in three weeks,

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:45.467
and we filmed it in 16 days.

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:48.400
-That's incredible.
That is not a lot of time.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.267
-No.
-You produced it

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:52.934
with your husband, but you also
involved your children.

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:54.267
-Yes. My son is a producer.

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:55.868
He's a music supervisor,
which, by the way,

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:57.200
he would freak out
about your band.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.334
I'm, like, back there rocking.

00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:00.200
I'm like,
he loves music so much.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.467
And he hooked me up
with Justin Biltonen

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:04.667
from 3 Doors Down
for country fans.

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:07.501
I sang with him last Tuesday
in Nashville.

00:03:07.501 --> 00:03:09.033
-Look at you.
-Uh-huh.

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:11.801
-Who's not a country singer?
-Come on.

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:13.501
I was on a rooftop singing --

00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:15.534
He wrote this song
"Heartache Rodeo".

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.300
-Okay. Were other people
on the rooftop?

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.434
Or was that something
you chose to do? Yeah.

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:21.200
-Well, my son booked it all.
-Oh, great.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.968
-And then my daughter, Sinead,
I gotta mention her --

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.667
I'll get punched in the face
when I get home --

00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:27.300
she -- she was the production
designer.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.934
And then my daughter,
Bernadette, she's a journalist.

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:31.167
So, she would help me the copy.
Anyway, we're all involved.

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:34.467
Even the dog. It's called
Trudy Ville, the town.

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:38.934
So we're all in on it.
And my son plays Chris, the cop.

00:03:38.934 --> 00:03:41.033
And, you know,
it was an insane asylum

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.534
because I'm not nocturnal.
-Yeah.

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:46.367
-And so, that's why,
like, whatever.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:49.968
But anyway,
I'd say there's rules.

00:03:49.968 --> 00:03:51.934
Okay? We have to have rules.
No one listens to me.

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:54.400
You know, we're an Irish family.
It's ridiculous.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.501
And so, I said,
"Do not wake me up.

00:03:56.501 --> 00:03:58.601
We're filming this movie.
I'm the producer.

00:03:58.601 --> 00:03:59.868
I have to, like,
get the porta potty --"

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:01.100
-You need your sleep, yeah.
-Yeah, I gotta make sure

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.167
the porta potties are clean.

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:03.767
My husband's getting food
for the crew,

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:05.400
and my son knock on the door
at 2:00 in the morning,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.968
"Um, can I talk to you
about that scene?"

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:09.701
I go, "I thought --
I thought we had a rule."

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:11.434
And he's like, "I'm sorry,

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.868
but I think we should say,
'Skedaddle, Valerie.'

00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:15.100
And then you should say,
'Skedaddle' back."

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.601
And I go, "That's funny.
Come in."

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:19.634
So -- So I look terrible
in the movie,

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:21.467
but I'm supposed to be
washed up, so it doesn't matter.

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:22.767
-Oh, yeah. There you go.
That's very smart.

00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:24.567
-I mean -- I mean,
what is happening under my eyes?

00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:26.067
I don't know.
-You, like, save money

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:28.200
on a makeup person
when you just stay up all night.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.501
-Completely. And I was --
And I was also a cougar wannabe.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:31.634
-Oh, yeah?
-Yeah, and so,

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:32.968
I'm supposed to try
to have an affair --

00:04:32.968 --> 00:04:35.934
well, try is --
heavy on the try --

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:38.133
with this Michael Oberholtzer,
who's a fabulous actor.

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:41.267
But I want to give a massive,
massive shout out.

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:44.667
Peter Macon, the guy
who plays the actor

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:47.801
who plays the prisoner --
-Yeah, the death row inmate.

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:49.734
-...it was an Oscar worthy
performance.

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:51.100
-That's fantastic.
You also --

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:52.200
I know you filmed it
in Syracuse,

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.400
which is where you're from.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.133
Did you actually
cast some locals

00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:57.167
that you knew back in the day?
-Oh, I did. I --

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:58.767
Matter of fact --
So the bar scenes,

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.400
which it didn't rain
the whole time,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.200
which is really unusual
for Syracuse,

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.634
and so, the only days
it rained was the bar.

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:07.167
And guess how I got
all my extras?

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:12.100
-How? Free beer?
-Thanks for asking.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.367
Close, I said to the class
of '79,

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.200
which not to date myself,

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.367
I said to my class, "Whoever
wants to be an extra can come

00:05:20.367 --> 00:05:21.868
and be in the bar scene."

00:05:21.868 --> 00:05:23.467
So that's my entire class
from '79.

00:05:23.467 --> 00:05:24.968
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:05:24.968 --> 00:05:26.601
-That's fantastic.
They all showed up for you?

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:27.934
-They all showed.
People flew in from all over.

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:29.501
And it's literally like,
I go to Nashville,

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:31.501
they come there,
I'm like, I have an entour--

00:05:31.501 --> 00:05:33.868
Like, when I was on "SNL",
I remember MC Hammer came

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:36.067
and he had, like, an entourage.
-Yeah.

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:37.601
-And now I have one.
-It just took you

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:40.000
a little bit longer
to reach your Hammer era.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.100
-Yeah.
Guess what else I have?

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.234
-What?
-So...

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:45.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:48.267
I probably shouldn't say this.
-You have the energy

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:49.968
of someone who probably
shouldn't say it.

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:51.467
-Should I not?
-Say it.

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:53.567
-I have a perv. I have a perv.

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:55.567
Somebody called me and, like,
did weird breathing.

00:05:55.567 --> 00:05:57.467
-You have your own perv?
-And I go, "I made it."

00:05:57.467 --> 00:06:01.133
-Oh, my God.
Siobhan, we're so --

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:03.334
we're so happy for you.
-Yes.

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:04.901
I went over. I went o--
Matter of fact,

00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:06.334
I went over
to "Saturday Night Live".

00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:08.734
You know, I had PTSD
when I came 'cause, you know...

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:09.901
-Yeah.
-Well, you know, I left

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:13.100
for "artistic differences" --
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:17.167
-...in '92.

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:19.868
Anyway, so I went over,
and I went on the stage,

00:06:19.868 --> 00:06:21.601
and the guys were so nice.

00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:24.400
And I said, "Live from New York,
it's Wednesday night.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.901
I'm on Seth Meyers show."

00:06:25.901 --> 00:06:27.667
-Well, there you go.
And here you are.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:29.334
And congratulations
on the movie.

00:06:29.334 --> 00:06:31.033
Everybody, check it out.
It's an independent film.

00:06:31.033 --> 00:06:32.767
These are the kind of movies
we need to go see.

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:35.734
You guys,
that is Siobhan Fallon Hogan.

00:06:35.734 --> 00:06:38.400
"Shelter in Solitude"
in theaters October 6th.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.601
Go see it and stick around.

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:41.133
We'll be right back
with music from Jungle.

